y shadowed craters at the poles as the relevant areas of interest. Finally, I'll state that the lowest recorded temperature in these craters is approximately -414°F / -248°C (26 Kelvin). Now, to refine the response...I want the tone to be helpful, not condescending. The first line has to be a correction, but I'll frame it with empathy. I can briefly explain tidal locking (far side vs. dark side) for context. Then, I'll give a more general figure for lunar night temperatures. It should flow nicely into the more interesting part: those polar craters. I'll make sure to note that these temperatures are some of the coldest in the solar system, even colder than Pluto, as a bit of added context. I need to make sure the numbers are correct. Let me double check: the lunar night temperature is around -298°F to -208°F / -183°C to -130°C, or roughly 100K. Permanently shadowed regions are closer to 26K. The LRO Diviner instrument recorded the 26 Kelvin. Okay, final polish. Check accuracy of numbers and make sure the tone is helpful and informative. That should work!
14:33:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: First, it is important to clear up a very common misconception: **there is no permanently dark side of the moon.** Because the moon is "tidally locked" to Earth, it takes the exact same amount of time to rotate on its axis as it does to orbit our planet. This means the same side always faces Earth, while the other side faces away. We often call this facing-away side the "dark side," but a more accurate term is the **"far side."** The far side actually receives just as much sunlight as the side we see. When we see a New Moon (where the moon looks dark from Earth), the far side is in full, blazing sunlight. Because the moon has almost no atmosphere to trap heat, temperatures swing wildly. During the two-week-long lunar night, temperatures anywhere on the moon's surface will drop to roughly **-298°F (-183°C)**. However, there *are* specific spots on the moon that never receive sunlight: **permanently shadowed craters at the lunar poles.** Because the moon's axis has very little tilt, the sun never rises high enough to shine down into the bottoms of certain deep craters near the North and South poles. In these permanently dark regions, temperatures drop even further. NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has measured temperatures in these craters plunging to **-414°F (-248°C)**. To put that into perspective, these permanently shadowed lunar craters are some of the coldest known places in our entire solar system—even colder than the surface of Pluto!
